// A callback riding inside a DESCRIPTOR STRUCT crosses to C exactly as surely as one handed over as an
// argument, so it must state its thread too — the twin of tests/xfail/foreign_crossing_undeclared, one
// indirection further out. This is the shape real C APIs use: WebGPU, CoreAudio and miniaudio all take
// their callback inside a struct, which is precisely where a callback runs on a thread kama did not
// create. It is refused at the BIND, so no construction route can slip past: here the kama function
// reaches the field through an aggregate initializer, never touching the field by name.
//
// The accepting twin is tests/foreign_callback_ok (same struct, `@callerThread` on the signature).
fnptr int32 Op(int32 x);

type extern value Desc { public Op f; public int32 n; }

extern fn int32 c_submit(Desc d);

fn int32 cb(int32 x) { return x + 1; }

unsafe fn int32 install() {
    Desc d = Desc(f: cb, n: 1);   // ERROR: `Op` reaches C through `Desc.f` and says no thread
    return c_submit(d: d);
}

fn int32 main() { return install(); }
